{"id":"h175","run":2,"role":"test_right","category":"science-chemistry","question":"Liquid nitrogen is an excellent bath for keeping temperatures around 77°K, its normal boiling point. What pressure would you need to maintain over the liquid nitrogen if you wanted to set the bath temperature at 85°K? Heat of vaporization is about 5560 (J / mole).\nIs the correct answer \"3.0 atm\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct pressure is about 2.3 atm, not 3.0 atm.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The Clausius-Clapeyron equation with the normal boiling point (77 K, 1 atm) as reference is the appropriate approach.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"With ΔHvap/R ≈ 669 K and 1/85 − 1/77 ≈ −0.001222 K⁻¹, ln(P2/P1) ≈ 0.818, giving P2 ≈ 2.26 atm.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Tabulated nitrogen vapor-pressure data give roughly 2.2–2.3 atm at 85 K, agreeing with the calculation.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Clausius-Clapeyron assumptions (ideal vapor, negligible liquid volume, constant ΔHvap) cause only a few percent error over this small range.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"That error is far too small to turn 2.3 atm into 3.0 atm.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"A pressure near 3 atm for an 8 K rise is plausible on its face and not absurd.","confidence":0.3,"lean":1,"theta":0.4712,"phi":-0.0},{"text":"The claimed 3.0 atm is about 30% too high and matches neither the calculation nor the data.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.95,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j"